@@ -159,6 +159,10 @@ Before any provider-mode command adjudication or Remote execution, Conversation
 
 `beforeDispatch(scope, { submissionId, requestId }, signal)` optionally durably associates the protected marker with the exact public DSH prompt request ID immediately before dispatch, including empty continuation prompts. The hook is awaited and its failure or cancellation blocks dispatch. The IDs do not establish native acceptance or durable entry commitment; authoritative reconciliation belongs to the native integration. Providers must check the supplied cancellation signal and their storage compare-and-swap authority at publication, so delayed writes cannot replace a newer owner or scope.
 
+Conversation installs through its public Cordis module lifecycle. Its controller requires and captures the public `fileUpload` service in the provider context; missing capability prevents activation.
+
+The default sink propagates exact Client-local `notDispatched` evidence from `SessionFace.prompt`. After the transient attempt settles, Conversation resolves only the matching request, original projection, retained binding, full scope, revision and unresolved marker as not submitted. Durable recovery publication precedes editability; failure or stale authority retains the frozen input. The busy notice, semantic text and ordered original references remain. Generic errors and unknown dispatched outcomes never clear a checkpoint, and recovery never sends input.
+
 Refresh publishes the semantic document, ordered originals, unresolved marker and read-only sending phase in one transaction, before synchronous observers can edit. It neither uploads originals nor submits anything. Transport failure, acceptance, provider withdrawal and refresh do not clear its marker. The integrating provider obtains an authoritative native outcome and explicitly calls `resolveDraftSubmission` with the current binding, full scope, revision, marker ID and `accepted` or `not-submitted`. Resolution persists before unlocking; stale, cancelled or failed resolution stays frozen. Accepted recovery removes only the attempted input and preserves a newer draft using explicit `ownsDraft` metadata, without matching text. Not-submitted recovery refuses to replace a separate newer draft; its owner must retain both snapshots for an explicit recovery choice. Not-submitted recovery returns the protected input for a later explicit send; original readiness remains invalid until an explicit restore obtains fresh bytes. No recovery decision automatically sends.
 
 Withdrawal aborts provider work, releases its runtime attachment handles, preserves text and original references, and invalidates readiness. A replacement registration retains observable identity. Consumers must provide recovery UI and an authoritative outcome lookup; neither a lost reply nor an absent native receipt establishes that an attempt was not submitted.
